-- set.iron -- Set[T: Hashable]: hash-based membership container (Phase 33 STDLIB-04)
-- Phase 33 OQ-01 / GA1: declares the generic Hashable BOUND on the element
-- parameter so the constraint enforcement in src/analyzer/typecheck.c
-- (check_generic_constraints, E0206) bites at every `Set[T]` instantiation:
-- `Set[Int]` / `Set[String]` are well-formed (Int/String satisfy Hashable via
-- the primitive carve-out), while `Set[NonHashable]` is a compile error naming
-- the unsatisfied bound.
--
-- SCOPE (Plan 33-02): TYPE + Hashable bound only — enough for the OQ-01
-- constraint check to fire on the check path. The full method surface
-- (Set.new / add / has / remove / len + the element drop/copy protocol over the
-- IRON_SET_IMPL backing in src/runtime/iron_runtime.h) is the Wave-4 container
-- rewrite (STDLIB-04 proper) and is intentionally NOT declared here.
--
-- The hash-based backing (Iron_Set_<T>, name-mangled per
-- src/runtime/iron_runtime.h:983) is preserved and unchanged by this plan.
--
-- `val inner` opaque storage (not `var`) so v3.0 E0264 does not fire on the
-- stdlib surface — mirrors arena.iron / box.iron / map.iron.

object Set[T: Hashable] {
    val inner: Int  -- opaque storage; the C runtime holds the real Iron_Set_<T>
}
